<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaMazon U'Mayim Acharonim — Halachah 4<br>Part 2b (§§ 13–16)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE FULL TRANSLATION · Based on <span class="lr">LM II:40</span></span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 13 — "<i>Your Stature Resembles a Palm Tree</i>"</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of Hashem praising Israel: <span class="v">"your stature resembles a palm tree"</span> — meaning that they are strong and hard and firm in their <span class="h">avodah</span> and do not allow themselves to be felled in any way. As Rashi explains there regarding the image of Nevuchadnetzar: they stood in their uprightness and did not bow down to it. For this is the praise and <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> of Israel, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — "<i>Komemiyus</i>" — Upright Stature Toward <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and I led you komemiyus [upright]"</span> — and Rashi explains: with upright stature. For this verse was said regarding <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, where Israel was heading. For all the journeys and paths and travels of Israel, and all the wars and conquests — all were to go and conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, as is understood and explained in the narratives of the entire Torah.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"and I led you komemiyus"</span> — with upright stature — the aspect of <span class="h">azus</span> and stubbornness, standing upright with great stubbornness to win the war and go and come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, which was the essential purpose of the Exodus, etc., as above. For it is impossible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — to win this war which is the essential totality of all the wars of the Jewish person — except through very great stubbornness, the aspect of <span class="v">"and I led you komemiyus."</span> The aspect of [what we say in prayer]: <span class="v">"and lead us speedily komemiyus to our land — speedily in our days, Amain."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — Eight Days of Chanukah = Eight Garments of the <i>Kohain Gadol</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore the miracle was through the <span class="h">kohanim</span>, who wear garments of <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"and you shall make holy garments for Aharon your brother, for kavod and for tiferes."</span> The essential one is the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span>, who wore the <span class="h">choshen</span> and <span class="h">aifod</span> upon which were inscribed the names of the children of Israel. There all the colors shone — the aspect of <span class="h">odem</span>, <span class="h">pitdah</span>, and <span class="h">barekes</span>, etc. — each stone having its own unique color — which are the totality of the <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> of Israel, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore there are eight days of Chanukah, corresponding to the eight garments of the <span class="h">Kehunah Gedolah</span> [High Priesthood], which are the totality of the <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> — from which the essential light of <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> [derives], as above. Therefore <span class="h">Parshas Bigdai Kehunah</span> is adjacent to the <span class="h">parshah</span> of <span class="v">"and they shall take for you olive oil,"</span> etc. — which is the aspect of <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="mx">"and they established these eight days of Chanukah to give thanks and praise to Your great Name."</span> For the <span class="h">hoda'os</span> [thanks] and <span class="h">hallel</span> [praise] are the <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> — that we praise and extol and glorify Him — through which Hashem is glorified and magnified and sanctified in all the worlds. This is the essential holiness of Chanukah, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 16 — <i>Parshas Vayehi Mikaitz</i> on Chanukah: Breaking the Desire for Money</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we read <span class="h">Parshas Vayehi Mikaitz</span> on Chanukah. For it is stated at the end of the aforementioned <span class="h">Torah</span> that speaks of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, etc. — and it concludes there: <span class="mx">"And there are still more matters in this — for 'a person does not die with half his desire in his hand.' It emerges that one's desire and will for money is never fulfilled, for one is always lacking, as above. But there is an aspect of doubled abundance — the aspect of 'and abundant silver to you,' which is a doubled expression. It emerges that with this abundance, one's desire is fulfilled, since it is doubled."</span> And [Rabbainu] did not conclude further — end quote.</div>

<div class="p">Now, Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> did not explain his words — what connection this matter has with everything he said previously regarding <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, etc. But what appears to my humble understanding, I will explain somewhat:</div>

<div class="p">The desire for money — i.e. the fact that "a person does not die with half his desire in his hand," and the more he has, the more he lacks — this is the aspect of the blemish of the eyes, in the aspect of <span class="v">"and also his eye is not satisfied with wealth"</span> (Koheles 4). And the blemish of the eyes is the aspect of the blemish of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. For the essential holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is drawn from the eyes — that gaze upon the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is comprised of all the <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> — through which the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is drawn.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is the source of blessing, for all blessings and abundances are drawn from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, as is known. For <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> receives its holiness from the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, as above. And by the <span class="h">tzadik</span> are all the blessings and abundances, in the aspect of <span class="v">"blessings upon the head of the tzadik,"</span> the aspect of <span class="v">"the tzadik is gracious and gives."</span> When one merits to receive the abundance of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and the <span class="h">tzadik</span> in completeness, certainly one has no lack at all. For regarding <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> it is written: <span class="v">"you shall not lack anything in it."</span> The abundance drawn from there has no lack, for <span class="v">"there Hashem commanded the blessing"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"I will pour out for you blessing without limit [ad b'li dai]"</span> — <span class="mx">"until your lips are worn out from saying 'enough'"</span> — the abundance is doubled and multiplied more than one desires.</div>

<div class="p">This is the opposite of "a person does not die with half his desire in his hand" — that one is always lacking. But the holy abundance of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is the aspect of doubled abundance, having no lack — the aspect of <span class="v">"blessing without limit."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the abundance of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, from which all the six weekdays are blessed — which is the aspect of <span class="h">lechem mishneh</span> [double bread], the aspect of doubled abundance. For <span class="h">Shabbos</span> and <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> are one aspect, as arranged in the seven <span class="h">havdalos</span> that our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> say in the Yerushalmi: "between the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and <span class="h">chutz la'Aretz</span>; between the seventh day and the six days of work." It emerges that the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, from whom all the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is drawn, merits doubled abundance, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of what is written regarding the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>: <span class="v">"therefore in their land they shall inherit double [mishneh]"</span> (Yeshayah 61). <span class="h">Mishneh</span> — this is doubled abundance, the aspect of <span class="h">lechem mishneh</span>. And this is <span class="v">"in their land — mishneh"</span> — "in their land" specifically — for the essential aspect of doubled abundance is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes in the <span class="h">Torah</span> <span class="v">"Tzavis Tzedek"</span> (<span class="lr">Siman 23</span>): the essential breaking of the desire for money is through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>. And whoever disputes the <span class="h">tzadik</span> falls into the desire for money, as he explains there on the verse <span class="v">"He drove out the enemy before you and said: 'Destroy!'"</span> When one connects to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, one draws from him the aspect of doubled abundance — the opposite of the desire for money, which is the aspect of "a person does not die with half his desire in his hand," as above.</div>

<div class="ts">
<div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — §§ 10–16 ✦</div>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§10</strong>: <em>Nesi'im</em> on Chanukah = <em>tzadikai hador</em> holding all the <em>his'pa'arus</em>. The <em>choshen</em> stones with their many colors = the variety of Israel's <em>his'pa'arus</em>. These colors on the banners conquered <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§11</strong>: Three levels of <em>ner Chanukah</em> = three levels of gazing: (1) Hashem's eyes (<em>ner ish u'vaiso</em>); (2) each individual's eyes after gazing at the <em>tzadik</em> (<em>mehadrin</em>); (3) the <em>tzadik</em> himself who is all <em>his'pa'arus</em> (<em>mehadrin min hamehadrin</em> = <em>mosif v'holaich</em> = Yosef). New <em>his'pa'arus</em> is the greatest — when those far away draw close. The Elder of the Seven Beggars: "very old yet very young." Chanukah from <em>chinuch</em> = initiation of the far ones.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§12</strong>: <em>Menorah mikshah</em> = stubbornness (<em>akshanus</em>). One must be a great stubborn person in avodas Hashem. <em>Azus</em> = the essential <em>his'pa'arus</em> — "Why Torah to Israel? Because they are fierce." Shiras HaYam was about reaching <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> through <em>his'pa'arus/azus</em>. The Menorah's western lamp = testimony that <em>Shechinah</em> rests on Israel. All its cups, knobs, flowers = varieties of <em>his'pa'arus</em>.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§§13-14</strong>: "Your stature like a palm tree" = standing firm, refusing to bow. "<em>Komemiyus</em>" = upright stature → <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§15</strong>: Eight days = eight garments of <em>Kohain Gadol</em> = totality of <em>his'pa'arus</em>. Adjacent to "take olive oil" = <em>ner Chanukah</em>. <em>Hallel</em> and <em>hoda'ah</em> = the <em>his'pa'arus</em> itself.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§16</strong>: <em>Parshas Mikaitz</em> on Chanukah. "No one dies with half his desire" = blemish of the eyes = blemish of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. The <em>tzadik</em> and <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> = source of all blessing = doubled abundance = <em>lechem mishneh</em> = <em>Shabbos</em>. Connecting to the <em>tzadik</em> breaks the desire for money.</p>
</div>

